brcm-dma.resource/--background--                    brcm-dma.resource/--background--

   NAME
	--background-- -- the versions of the resource, and how they are written here

   FUNCTION
	The calls of this resource are described one by one below. This entry says
	since when each exists, and how a change is told, as the autodocs of the
	system do (see exec.library/AllocPooled or StackSwap).

	A call that was added after the first version of the resource has its
	version at the end of the line of its NAME, like "(V0.2)": the number is
	the version of the resource in which it appeared. A tag that came later is
	marked the same way where it is described. A change in what a call does is
	written in its FUNCTION or NOTES text, "As of V0.2 ..." and "Prior to V0.2
	...". A call without a version in its NAME has been there since 0.1.

	The version is 0.x: the interface is not public yet and may change until a
	program really uses the resource and confirms that it can be used as these
	autodocs say.

   VERSIONS
	The history comes from the repository of the project.

	0.1   2026-10-06  First version (revision 1): BDMA_OpenClientTagList,
	                  BDMA_CloseClient, BDMA_AllocJobTagList,
	                  BDMA_AddJobTagList, BDMA_SetJobTagList, BDMA_StartJob,
	                  BDMA_WaitJob, BDMA_CheckJob, BDMA_AbortJob,
	                  BDMA_FreeJob, BDMA_QueryJobTagList and
	                  BDMA_QueryInfoTagList, with the forms that take the tags
	                  as arguments.
	     2026-10-08  BDMA_ResetStatistics added at the end of the table, with no
	                  new revision. The order of the vectors was changed the
	                  same day, also with no new revision: a program built
	                  before must be built again.
	0.2   2026-10-16  BDMA_AddRangeTagList, BDMA_AddRangeTags and
	                  BDMA_RemRange: a client declares a range of memory that
	                  exec does not know, such as the framebuffer of HDMI1.
	0.3   2026-10-16  BDMA_WaitClient, the tag BDJ_Detached and BDJ_ErrorAddress
	                  for the jobs, BDI_RangeLo and BDI_RangeHi for the
	                  window of the ranges. Revision 2 was not committed on its
	                  own, it came in the same commit.
	                  Later the same day, at the same revision: the cache is
	                  looked after by CachePreDMA() and CachePostDMA() only,
	                  rectangles whose rows follow each other are started as
	                  one row, and rectangles with a gap between the rows are
	                  built by a short loop. These make the start of a job
	                  cheaper and change nothing in what a call does.

   NOTES
	lib_Version and lib_Revision of the resource come from the same place as
	the id string, the version of the project: 0.3 gives lib_Version 0 and
	lib_Revision 3. BDI_Version gives them as (version << 16) | revision.
	In the builds made after the commit of revisions 2 and 3 and before the
	version came from the project, the id string said 0.1 while lib_Revision
	was 3: the revision was written by hand and the project version was left
	at 0.1. A resource with the id string "brcm-dma 0.1" and lib_Revision 3 is
	one of those; nothing else differs.

	The vectors of revisions 2 and 3 are the last ones of the table. A program
	that needs BDMA_WaitClient (V0.3) tests lib_Revision >= 3, or
	BDI_Version.

   SEE ALSO
	exec.library/OpenResource(), brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_QueryInfoTagList()

brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AbortJob                     brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AbortJob

   NAME
	BDMA_AbortJob -- stop a job

   SYNOPSIS
	BDMA_AbortJob( job )
	a0

	VOID BDMA_AbortJob(struct BDMAJob *);

   FUNCTION
	As AbortIO() does for a request. A job that runs is stopped with the sequence of the hardware, one that waits in the queue is removed; the job is
	replied to its reply port with BDERR_ABORTED. A job that is over is left alone. A job from BDMA_AddJobTagList() that was replied is taken off the
	port and freed by this call.

	What the job had already written stays written.

	When a slice of the job is being armed at that moment (its task was preempted, or it is in an interrupt) the reply comes a moment later, from that
	slice: follow the call with BDMA_WaitJob(), as with AbortIO() and WaitIO().

   INPUTS
	job             a job

   RESULTS
	none

   EXAMPLES
	BDMA_AbortJob(job);

   SEE ALSO
	brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_WaitJob(), brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_CheckJob()

brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AddJobTagList           brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AddJobTagList

   NAME
	BDMA_AddJobTagList -- describe a job and start it, for one use

   SYNOPSIS
	job = BDMA_AddJobTagList( client, tagList )
	d0                        a0      a1

	struct BDMAJob *BDMA_AddJobTagList(struct BDMAClient *, CONST struct TagItem *);

   FUNCTION
	BDMA_AllocJobTagList() and BDMA_StartJob() in one call. The job is freed by the BDMA_WaitJob() or the BDMA_AbortJob() that ends it: it is for a job
	that is used once. A job that BDJ_NoWait refused is replied at once, BDMA_WaitJob() then gives BDERR_BUSY.

	With BDJ_Detached nobody waits for the job and nobody frees it: the resource does, once it is over, and BDMA_WaitClient() sleeps until all of
	them are done and gives the first error. The job that the call gives back then only means "accepted": do not wait for it, do not abort it, do not
	use it (it may be freed by any later call of the client). It is the way to start many rectangles without keeping them in an array.

   INPUTS
	client          a client, from BDMA_OpenClientTagList()
	tagList         the tags of BDMA_AllocJobTagList(), and

	BDJ_Detached    BOOL (V0.3), the job is let go as described above

   RESULTS
	job             a job, or NULL: BDJ_ErrorCode says why

   EXAMPLES
	job = BDMA_AddJobTags(client, BDJ_Src, src, BDJ_Dst, dst, BDJ_Length, 4096, TAG_DONE);
	if (job != NULL)
	    error = BDMA_WaitJob(job);

   NOTES
	A task context (the tags are parsed here).

   SEE ALSO
	brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AllocJobTagList(), brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_StartJob(), brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_WaitJob()

brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AddJobTags                 brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AddJobTags

   NAME
	BDMA_AddJobTags -- BDMA_AddJobTagList() with the tags as arguments

   SYNOPSIS
	job = BDMA_AddJobTags( client, tag1Type, ... )
	d0                     a0      a1

	struct BDMAJob *BDMA_AddJobTags(struct BDMAClient *, ULONG, ...);

   FUNCTION
	The same as BDMA_AddJobTagList(), the tags follow as arguments (pairs of a tag and its value, ended by TAG_DONE). It is not a function of the
	resource: it is generated inline from the SFD, as OpenScreenTags() is.

   SEE ALSO
	brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AddJobTagList()

brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AddRangeTagList       brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AddRangeTagList

   NAME
	BDMA_AddRangeTagList -- declare a range of memory for the jobs of a client (V0.2)

   SYNOPSIS
	range = BDMA_AddRangeTagList( client, address, size, tagList )
	d0                            a0      d0       d1    a1

	struct BDMARange *BDMA_AddRangeTagList(struct BDMAClient *, ULONG, ULONG, CONST struct TagItem *);

   FUNCTION
	A job is accepted only in memory that the resource knows to be safe for the DMA: the fast RAM that exec knows, and the RTG memory
	(/emu68 vc4-mem). This call opens one more place for the jobs of ONE client: memory of the GPU that the client owns, for instance the
	framebuffer that the firmware gave it (mailbox ALLOCATE_BUFFER, ARM address = bus address & 0x3FFFFFFF). A job of that client may then
	read and write it, with the same DMA addresses as for the rest of the RAM; the jobs of the other clients still get BDERR_RANGE there.

	The range is checked, and refused with BDERR_RANGE when:
	 - it is not inside the window of the GPU: the part of the first gigabyte that the device tree leaves out of /memory (it starts at the end
	   of the last /memory entry below 1 GB and ends at 0x40000000; BDMA_QueryInfoTagList() gives it as BDI_RangeLo and BDI_RangeHi). The RAM of
	   the ARM is not opened by this call, it is known to exec;
	 - it meets the RTG memory, or the memory that the resource keeps for its own channels (control blocks, buffer, constant of the fills).
	It is refused with BDERR_OVERLAP when it meets a range of any client, and with BDERR_ARGS when the address or the size is not a multiple of
	4096 bytes, the size is 0, the range wraps around, BDR_ReadOnly and BDR_WriteOnly are both given, or BDJ_Strict is given with a tag that this
	version does not know.

	The caller answers for the memory: the resource cannot know when the firmware takes it back. Remove the range (BDMA_RemRange()) BEFORE the
	buffer is released, or close the client: closing a client removes its ranges.

   INPUTS
	client          a client, from BDMA_OpenClientTagList()
	address         the first byte, a multiple of 4096 (the CPU address, which is the DMA address)
	size            the bytes, a multiple of 4096
	tagList         BDR_Cached      (BOOL) the CPU caches this memory: the jobs do the cache maintenance on it (default: not cached, as the
	                                RTG memory, and as the framebuffer of the firmware)
	                BDR_ReadOnly    (BOOL) jobs may read the range and not write it
	                BDR_WriteOnly   (BOOL) jobs may write the range and not read it
	                BDR_Name        (STRPTR) a name for the tools that list the owners (copied, 31 characters)
	                BDJ_Strict, BDJ_ErrorCode as for BDMA_AllocJobTagList()

   RESULTS
	range           a range, or NULL: BDJ_ErrorCode says why (BDERR_UNAVAILABLE when no engine runs on this machine)

   EXAMPLES
	range = BDMA_AddRangeTags(client, (ULONG)fb.pixels & ~4095, (fb.size + 4095) & ~4095, BDR_Name, (ULONG)"fb1", TAG_DONE);
	if (range != NULL)
	{
	    job = BDMA_AddJobTags(client, BDJ_Src, picture, BDJ_Dst, fb.pixels, BDJ_Length, bytes, TAG_DONE);
	    ...
	    BDMA_RemRange(range);
	}

   NOTES
	A task context. The jobs that were checked against the range keep a mark: after BDMA_RemRange() they are refused by BDMA_StartJob()
	(BDERR_RANGE) until BDMA_SetJobTagList() checks them again.

   SEE ALSO
	brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_RemRange(), brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AllocJobTagList()

