brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AllocJobTagList       brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AllocJobTagList

   NAME
	BDMA_AllocJobTagList -- describe a job

   SYNOPSIS
	job = BDMA_AllocJobTagList( client, tagList )
	d0                          a0      a1

	struct BDMAJob *BDMA_AllocJobTagList(struct BDMAClient *, CONST struct TagItem *);

   FUNCTION
	The tags are parsed and checked once, here, in a task context; the engine starts with the first job. The job belongs to the client until
	BDMA_FreeJob(). BDMA_StartJob() queues it, as many times as wanted; BDMA_SetJobTagList() changes it in between.

	A job is a copy (BDJ_Src), a fill (BDJ_FillValue) or, with BDJ_Move, a move by whole rows; with BDJ_Rows a rectangle.

   INPUTS
	client          a client
	tagList         the tags of the job

	BDJ_Src         ULONG, address of the first byte to read (not with BDJ_FillValue)
	BDJ_Dst         ULONG, address of the first byte to write (required)
	BDJ_FillValue   ULONG, a pixel written again and again (instead of BDJ_Src): its BDJ_FillBytes low bytes, in the byte order of the memory (the long
	                word as the 68k writes it)
	BDJ_FillBytes   ULONG, the bytes of that pixel: 1, 2 or 4 (default 4). The pattern starts again with every row; BDJ_Length is a multiple of it, the address
	                and the pitch are free
	BDJ_Length      ULONG, bytes of a row (required; for a fill a multiple of BDJ_FillBytes)
	BDJ_Rows        ULONG, number of rows, default 1, 65535 at most
	BDJ_SrcPitch    ULONG, bytes between two source rows, default BDJ_Length
	BDJ_DstPitch    ULONG, bytes between two destination rows, default BDJ_Length
	BDJ_Move        BOOL, the areas may overlap (a scroll, a shift to the side); the pitches must be equal; the result is that of a copy of the whole
	                rectangle that reads everything before it writes (a shift inside a row goes through a buffer of the channel, at about half the
	                rate); BDERR_OVERLAP only when a row does not fit in that buffer (256 KB)
	BDJ_NoCache     BOOL, no cache maintenance: for memory that the CPU does not cache, or that the caller looked after
	BDJ_NoWait      BOOL, BDERR_BUSY (when the job is started) instead of waiting, when every channel is busy or the job conflicts with a job that runs or is queued
	BDJ_Priority    ULONG, BDPRI_LOW, BDPRI_NORMAL or BDPRI_HIGH, default the priority of the client
	BDJ_ReplyPort   struct MsgPort *, where the job is replied when it is over, default the port of the client
	BDJ_Classes     ULONG, the classes of channel the job may run on (BDCLASS_DMA40, BDCLASS_NORMAL, BDCLASS_LITE), default every class that can do it
	BDJ_Timeout     ULONG, microseconds: the longest a slice of the job (a megabyte at most) may stay on a channel; default 1000000, at most 10000000 (0 and larger values give 10000000: no job waits for a stuck channel forever). Over it the
	                channel is stopped and the job is replied with BDERR_TIMEOUT. A watchdog on the vertical blank checks it: the resolution is a tick (20 ms)
	BDJ_Strict      BOOL, a tag that this version does not know is BDERR_ARGS instead of being ignored
	BDJ_ErrorCode   ULONG *, receives BDERR_OK, or the reason why the job could not be made
	BDJ_ErrorAddress ULONG * (V0.3), receives, for BDERR_RANGE, the address of the block that was refused (the destination is looked at first, then the
	                source), and 0 for any other outcome

   RESULTS
	job             a job, or NULL

	BDERR_UNAVAILABLE   no engine, or no channel of the classes allowed
	BDERR_ARGS          a missing, wrong or incompatible tag, a fill whose BDJ_FillBytes is not 1, 2 or 4 or does not divide BDJ_Length, a pitch smaller than the length
	BDERR_RANGE         an address that is not memory, or that the DMA of the Pi does not reach: anything that is not fast memory (MEMF_FAST), the Chip RAM and everything below 2 MB, the memory of the 24 bit space (MEMF_24BITDMA) except the RAM board of Emu68 (z2ram), the range from 0xA00000 to 0xFFFFFF (PCMCIA, CIAs, chipset, clock, ROM), the table of the vectors of the 68k (VBR), an expansion board that is not the RAM of Emu68. A transfer there would freeze the machine. The memory of the GPU that the client declared with BDMA_AddRangeTagList() is accepted for its own jobs (and for the direction that the range allows).
	BDERR_OVERLAP       the source and the destination overlap (without BDJ_Move, or a row of more than 256 KB with it)
	BDERR_TOOBIG        too many rows, or a row of a gigabyte

	Once started, a job can end with BDERR_BUSY (BDJ_NoWait), BDERR_ABORTED, BDERR_TIMEOUT or BDERR_HW: BDMA_WaitJob() gives it.

   EXAMPLES
	job = BDMA_AllocJobTags(client, BDJ_Src, src, BDJ_Dst, dst, BDJ_Length, 4096, BDJ_ErrorCode, &error, TAG_DONE);

   NOTES
	The jobs are run as if they were done one by one in the order in which they are started: a job that writes what an earlier one reads or writes
	waits for it; jobs that touch different memory run together, on the channels there are. A job of more than a megabyte runs in slices, so that a
	more urgent one does not wait for its end.

	A rectangle costs the CPU a control block for each row (about 0.6 us: a picture of 1080 rows of 1280 bytes, 645 us to start). When the rows follow
	each other in the source and in the destination (BDJ_SrcPitch and BDJ_DstPitch equal to BDJ_Length, or left out), the job is done as one row of all
	the bytes (173 us for the same 1.4 MB, whatever the number of rows); nothing changes for the caller. A BDJ_Move keeps its rows.

	Unless BDJ_NoCache, the data cache is written back before the transfer and dropped after it, before the reply: the CPU reads what the DMA wrote.
	The memory of the RTG board (BDI_VideoBase, BDI_VideoSize), which the CPU does not cache, is left alone: a job inside it needs no BDJ_NoCache.

	A task context (utility.library is opened here).

   SEE ALSO
	brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_StartJob(), brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_SetJobTagList(), brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_FreeJob()

