brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_StartJob                     brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_StartJob

   NAME
	BDMA_StartJob -- queue a job

   SYNOPSIS
	BDMA_StartJob( job )
	a0

	VOID BDMA_StartJob(struct BDMAJob *);

   FUNCTION
	Queues the job, described by BDMA_AllocJobTagList(). It allocates nothing and parses nothing: a software interrupt may call it (the next block of a
	transfer, for instance). When the job is over it is replied to its reply port, with the result in bj_Error and the bytes in bj_Done. With
	BDJ_NoWait and every channel busy it is replied at once, with BDERR_BUSY. A job that is in flight is left alone.

	A big job is run in slices of about 1 MB. When its reads and its writes do not overlap (a copy, a fill, a rectangle; not a move inside one
	bitmap) and the second channel has nothing else to do, two slices of the job run on the two channels at once: the transfer is up to 1.6 times
	faster. The second channel is only lent: a job that arrives meanwhile waits at most one slice.

   INPUTS
	job             a job

   RESULTS
	none; the result comes with the reply (BDMA_WaitJob(), or GetMsg() on the port)

   NOTES
	The reply of an earlier run must have been taken off the port before the job is started again.

   SEE ALSO
	brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_WaitJob(), brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AbortJob()

brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_WaitClient                   brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_WaitClient

   NAME
	BDMA_WaitClient -- sleep until the jobs of a client are over (V0.3)

   SYNOPSIS
	error = BDMA_WaitClient( client )
	d0                       a0

	LONG BDMA_WaitClient(struct BDMAClient *);

   FUNCTION
	Sleeps (the task is asleep, not looping: the 68k is free for the others) until none of the jobs of the client is in flight, then frees the
	jobs that were started with BDJ_Detached and gives the first error among them since the last call: BDERR_OK, or the reason of the first one
	that ended with another result (BDERR_ABORTED, BDERR_TIMEOUT, BDERR_HW, BDERR_BUSY, BDERR_RANGE). The error is then forgotten.

	The other jobs of the client (BDMA_AllocJobTagList(), BDMA_AddJobTagList() without BDJ_Detached) are waited for and left alone: their reply,
	their result and their freeing are still the client's, by BDMA_WaitJob(). A job that was refused when it was made is not a job of the client
	and is not counted here: BDMA_AddJobTagList() gave NULL and BDJ_ErrorCode its reason.

	A client that starts detached jobs and never calls this function does not pile them up: BDMA_AddJobTagList() frees the ones that are over
	when 32 of them wait in the list, and BDMA_CloseClient() frees the rest.

   INPUTS
	client          a client

   RESULTS
	error           BDERR_OK, or the first error of the detached jobs; BDERR_ARGS for NULL; BDERR_BUSY when the task has no signal left to sleep on

   EXAMPLES
	for each rectangle that has to be copied:
	    BDMA_AddJobTags(client, BDJ_Src, s, BDJ_Dst, d, BDJ_Length, w, BDJ_Rows, h, BDJ_SrcPitch, sp, BDJ_DstPitch, dp, BDJ_Detached, TRUE, TAG_DONE);
	...the CPU does something else...
	error = BDMA_WaitClient(client);
	if (error != BDERR_OK)
	    ... copy the rectangles again with the CPU ...

   NOTES
	For the task that owns the client (its reply port is that task's): another task that frees the jobs of the client meanwhile is not looked after.

   SEE ALSO
	brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AddJobTagList(), brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_WaitJob(), brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_CloseClient()

brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_WaitJob                       brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_WaitJob

   NAME
	BDMA_WaitJob -- sleep until a job is over

   SYNOPSIS
	error = BDMA_WaitJob( job )
	d0                    a0

	LONG BDMA_WaitJob(struct BDMAJob *);

   FUNCTION
	As WaitIO() does for a request: sleeps until the job is replied and gives the result. The task is asleep, not looping: the 68k is free for the others.
	Any task may wait for any job: the owner of a signal port sleeps on the signal of the port, any other task on a signal of its own that the reply wakes.
	A signal port holds the reply until it is taken: this call takes it (use this call or GetMsg() on such a port, not both); a port that wakes a software
	interrupt has its reply taken in that interrupt. A second call gives the same result again. A job from BDMA_AddJobTagList() is freed here.

   INPUTS
	job             a job that was started

   RESULTS
	error           BDERR_OK or the reason: BDERR_BUSY (BDJ_NoWait, or no signal left to sleep on), BDERR_ABORTED, BDERR_TIMEOUT, BDERR_HW; BDERR_ARGS for a job that
	                was not started

   NOTES
	A job whose port wakes a software interrupt is taken in that interrupt with GetMsg(); another task may still wait for it here.

   SEE ALSO
	brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_StartJob(), brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_CheckJob(), brcm-dma.resource/BDMA_AbortJob()
